  m_Name: ShellandTubeHeatExchangerData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: Shell and Tube Heat Exchanger
  engineDescription: A Shell-and-Tube Heat Exchanger is a thermal energy-transfer
    device used to transfer heat between two fluids without directly mixing them.
    Its main components include the shell, tubes, tube sheets, inlet and outlet nozzles,
    baffles, channel heads, and supporting structures. During operation, one fluid
    flows through the tubes while the other fluid flows around the outside of the
    tubes within the shell. Heat is transferred through the tube walls from the fluid
    at higher temperature to the fluid at lower temperature. Baffles are commonly
    provided inside the shell to guide the shell-side fluid and increase turbulence,
    thereby improving heat transfer. Shell-and-tube heat exchangers are widely used
    in power plants, chemical processing, petroleum refineries, refrigeration systems,
    HVAC equipment, and industrial process plants. From an engineering perspective,
    understanding heat-transfer coefficients, temperature differences, fluid flow
    patterns, pressure drop, fouling, thermal expansion, material selection, corrosion,
    and maintenance is essential for achieving efficient and long-term heat exchanger
    performance.
  engineCategory: Engineering
